<h1>And Programming is in Session…</h1> <p>As some of you may recall, last week I started work on the FT8 encoder block. This week, while testing some code, I suddenly had a realization: logging should be something that's standardized in GNU Radio, I shouldn't need to reinvent the wheel here.</p> <h2>Enter d_logger</h2> <p>I present to you: <strong>d_logger</strong>. In C++ within a GNU Radio block, d_logger is a member with multiple levels: trace, debug, info, warn, error, and critical. I found these categories to be a reasonable and logical way of handling different types of messages.</p> <p>The "debug" category is obviously the one I'll be using frequently right now, but I already see uses for the other levels. For example, I'm using "warn" when the input has a double space or invalid character that I need to remove, while "error" is for when there's no message input at all -making it impossible to send anything since there's no message to work with.</p> <h2>When Things Go Sideways</h2> <p>But before I could actually implement and test this incredibly useful functionality, a wrench was thrown into the mix. To test the code I'm writing, I've been importing the module completely into Python with plans to use d_logger. However, d_logger requires <strong>GNU Radio 3.10</strong>, while everything I'd created so far was in version 3.8.</p> <p>So I updated everything, expecting life to go on simply. However, as is always the case when you expect things to go smoothly, everything crashed. Suddenly I was faced with this error:</p> <p><strong>ImportError: generic_type: type "encoder" referenced unknown base type "gr::sync_block"</strong></p> <p>This would turn out to be a major pain.</p> <h2>The pybind11 Rabbit Hole</h2> <p>I started with just the boilerplate code and was already getting this error. The culprit? <strong>pybind11</strong>. In GNU Radio 3.8, SWIG was used instead of pybind11, so I hadn't encountered this problem before.</p> <p>For those unaware, both SWIG and pybind11 accomplish the same task: they create bindings between C++ code and Python, allowing you to call C++ functions directly from Python (which was my goal for testing). This is obviously important and useful, though for me it introduced quite a bit of iterative debugging.</p> <p>The module was building and installing successfully, but importing it in Python caused crashes. I tried everything: adding missing GNU Radio headers directly to the bindings, modifying the PYTHONPATH, multiple rebuilds with different library and Python versions, creating multiple conda environments built with different versions, and even changing the installation path to the conda environment compared to the system.</p> <h2>The Solution</h2> <p>It turns out the problem was version compatibility: the pybind version required for GNU Radio was older, but those older versions don't work with the Python version I was using. Once I discovered this, the fix was straightforward—just <strong>downgrade Python</strong>.</p> <h2>Looking Ahead</h2> <p>For next week, I'll be submitting a PR with a significant portion of the preprocessing complete. We shall see how that goes!</p> <p>Continue following to see what adventures this newbie GNU Radio contributor goes on next.</p> <p><strong>Reference:</strong> <a href="https://wiki.gnuradio.org/index.php/Logging">GNU Radio Logging Documentation</a></p>

